window.docContents[146].resolve({"/Distance/#section-diameter-and-radius-of-the-hypercube":{"contents":"Diameters add under box products (ediam_boxProd) and the complete graph on Bool\nhas diameter 1 (ediam_top), so by induction the diameter of Q[n] is n. The\nbase case is the one-vertex graph Q[0], whose diameter is 0\n(ediam_eq_zero_of_subsingleton), conveniently we already gave HypercubeVert 0 a\nSubsingleton instance, hypercubeZeroSubsingleton.\n\nlemma ediam_hypercube {n : ℕ} : Q[n].ediam = n := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => exact ediam_eq_zero_of_subsingleton\n  | succ n ih => rw [hypercube_succ, ediam_boxProd, ih, ediam_top, Nat.cast_succ]\n\n\nlemma diam_hypercube {n : ℕ} : Q[n].diam = n := by\n  rw [SimpleGraph.diam, ediam_hypercube, ENat.toNat_natCast]\n\n\nSince the hypercube is vertex-transitive (isVertexTransitive_hypercube) its radius\nequals its diameter, and every vertex is central.\n\nlemma radius_hypercube {n : ℕ} : Q[n].radius = n := by\n  rw [(isVertexTransitive_hypercube n).radius_eq_ediam, ediam_hypercube]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Eccentricity, Diameter and Radius","header":"11.2.3. Diameter and Radius of the Hypercube","id":"/Distance/#section-diameter-and-radius-of-the-hypercube"},"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-graphs":{"contents":"The extremal graphs are the Turán graphs. Mathlib defines turanGraph n r on\nFin n, and we introduce the notation T[n, r].\n\n#source turanGraph\n\n\nT[n, r] is the Turán graph turanGraph n r: the complete r-partite graph on\nFin n whose parts are the residue classes modulo r./-- T[n, r] is the Turán graph turanGraph n r: the complete r-partite graph on\nFin n whose parts are the residue classes modulo r. -/\nscoped notation \"T[\" n \", \" r \"]\" => turanGraph n r\n\n\nThe Turán graph T[10, 3]\n\nTwo vertices of T[n, r] are adjacent iff they have different residues modulo\nr, so it is the complete r-partite graph whose parts are the residue classes.\nThese parts are balanced: any two differ in size by at most one, since the residue\nclasses of 0, …, n - 1 modulo r have sizes ⌊n / r⌋ or ⌈n / r⌉. In Mathlib's\nterms they form an equipartition (Finpartition.IsEquipartition, defined using\nSet.EquitableOn). For example, when r divides n the Turán graph T[n, r] is\nisomorphic to Mathlib's completeEquipartiteGraph r (n / r), which has r parts\nof size n / r.\n\n#check completeEquipartiteGraph.turanGraph\n\n\nThe case r = 0 is a junk value. Since a % 0 = a in Lean, any two distinct\nvertices are adjacent in T[n, 0], so T[n, 0] = K[n]. Most results about Turán\ngraphs assume 0 < r.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : T[n, 0] = K[n] := by\n  simp\n\n\nFor instance, a graph is 1-clique-free iff it has no vertices, so T[n, 0] is not\n1-clique-free when 0 < n.\n\n#check cliqueFree_one\n#check turanGraph_cliqueFree -- 0 < r → T[n, r].CliqueFree (r + 1)\n\n\nThe number of edges of T[n, r] is called turanNumber n r. Turán graphs have\ndecidable adjacency, so we can compute it.\n\n#eval turanNumber 10 3 -- 33\n#check turanNumber_eq\n\n\nIn particular turanNumber n r is at most (1 - 1 / r) * n ^ 2 / 2.\n\n#check mul_turanNumber_le -- 2 * r * turanNumber n r ≤ (r - 1) * n ^ 2\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Turán's Theorem, Stability and Asymptotics","header":"13.2.1. Turán Graphs","id":"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-graphs"}});